  "spinAero": {
    "controlSpoilerK": 0.6,
    "controlSpoilerKSource": "A deflected control on a BROADSIDE surface acts as a spoiler on the separated wake, not as a flap: adds k*sin(delta)*|sin(alpha_chord)|*q*S normal to the FIXED surface's own plane, over hinged tail strips only, ramped in over 20 deg from controlSpoilerOnsetDeg. 0.6 is the PITTS' value and is carried unchanged, not refitted per type: it was fitted there on 2026-11-10 to Ty flying a clip of his own Pitts flat spin (\"the elevator literally does nothing in the spin\"), and pitts-s2b-like.json holds the sweep it came from - elevator authority at alpha 80 of 3.7 deg/s2 at k 0, 15.5 at 0.3, 27.4 at 0.6, 43.4 at 1.0, where 1.0 leaves the curve nearly flat from 30 to 90 deg and a plate should not be. WHY THIS TYPE HAS IT NOW. It shipped on the Pitts and the Extra alone, and that was STAGING rather than a finding: the argument for the term is about the SECTION - post-stall the section law is Cd_max*sin(theta), which is flat at broadside, so a strip model gives a control on a broadside surface a direction to point its force in and no magnitude to command - and when this type's horizontal tail became a flat plate on 2026-11-10 (b7396cd0: stall 9 deg, Cl_max 0.90, blend 44) that argument started applying to it too. Worse, the controlSpoilerOnsetDeg 10 that came with the plate was INERT here, because the spoiler block in AeroModel.Compute is gated on controlSpoilerK > 0.0: the onset was moving the start of a term multiplied by zero, and the hole it was meant to close was being closed by the 44 deg blend alone. Ty 2026-11-10, shown exactly that and the argument that the restriction to two types was staging: \"Do it\". MEASURED ON THIS TYPE (SpoilerFleetProbe, SPOILER_FLEET=1). Authority is the pitching moment the stick's whole travel commands at 30 m/s with the wind turned to the body alpha asked for, as a fraction of its own alpha 0 value; the floor is the worst of it over alpha 10-60 at a 0.5 deg step. Floor 0.158 at alpha 40 -> 0.212 at alpha 28; alpha 45 0.180 -> 0.271. IT COSTS THE PITCH DAMPING NOTHING, and for a mechanical reason rather than a lucky one: the term carries sin(gain*deflEff) and is gated on a non-zero deflection, so it cannot appear in a controls-neutral quantity, and dM/dq is one. VERIFIED rather than asserted, because if it had moved then the gate would not be what we think it is: the worst dM/dq over alpha 10-60 is BIT-IDENTICAL at k 0 and k 0.6 on all eleven configs that gained it and on the two that already had it. Vso AND THE TRIM ARE UNMOVED TOO, which was not obvious and had to be measured rather than argued: unlike stabHighAlphaK's 60-90 deg band, this term opens at 10 deg of the strip's OWN flow, and a tail strip with the stick near its aft stop at the stall is not self-evidently below that. It is - measured on the Stearman at its own Vso, the stabiliser's local angle is 2.74 deg against the 10 deg onset, because the downwash (3.49 deg there) and the stabiliser's own negative incidence take it out of the body alpha - so the ramp never opens in any ordinary flight condition and every type's Vso, trim alpha and trim elevator come out unchanged to the digit. WHAT IT DOES CHANGE is high-alpha pitch authority and therefore the spin, which is the point, and nothing below the ramp.",
    "controlSpoilerOnsetDeg": 10.0,
    "controlSpoilerOnsetDegSource": "The spoiler ramp starts at the SECTION'S OWN STALL. 30 deg was chosen when every hStab was a naca0012 (stall 14 deg); a flat plate stalls at 9-12, which left alpha 20-34 with no table effectiveness and no spoiler yet - and that band WAS the hole in the elevator authority. Worth the whole difference: authority floor 0.165 at onset 30 against 0.249 at onset 10. Ty 2026-11-10 accepted the plate knowing this moves the term he judged the Pitts' spin on."
  }
